WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.281
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.581 --> 00:00:09.304
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.604 --> 00:00:14.949
Unit ten: Citation Ethics. Today you will use gerund phrases as sentence subjects.

4
00:00:15.249 --> 00:00:17.890
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:22.020
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:23.838
Plagiarism.

7
00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:29.044
Plagiarism means presenting another person's work or ideas as your own.

8
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.532
Self-plagiarism is also considered misconduct.

9
00:00:33.117 --> 00:00:35.955
Your turn. Say: Plagiarism.

10
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:39.940
Plagiarism.

11
00:00:40.683 --> 00:00:41.859
Attribution.

12
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:47.195
Attribution means the act of crediting the originator of an idea or work.

13
00:00:47.783 --> 00:00:50.816
Correct attribution is required even for images.

14
00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:54.011
Your turn. Say: Attribution.

15
00:00:56.867 --> 00:00:57.937
Attribution.

16
00:00:58.683 --> 00:01:00.300
Intellectual property.

17
00:01:01.283 --> 00:01:05.164
Intellectual property means creations of the mind protected by law.

18
00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.597
Patents and copyright protect intellectual property.

19
00:01:09.183 --> 00:01:12.207
Your turn. Say: Intellectual property.

20
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:16.671
Intellectual property.

21
00:01:17.417 --> 00:01:18.453
Paraphrase.

22
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:23.493
Paraphrase means to restate an idea fully in your own words and structure.

23
00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:27.197
Merely swapping synonyms does not paraphrase a text.

24
00:01:27.783 --> 00:01:30.761
Your turn. Say: Paraphrase.

25
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:34.648
Paraphrase.

26
00:01:35.383 --> 00:01:36.512
Integrity.

27
00:01:37.517 --> 00:01:41.757
Integrity means the quality of being honest and consistent in principles.

28
00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:45.452
Academic integrity underpins the value of a degree.

29
00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:48.505
Your turn. Say: Integrity.

30
00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:52.414
Integrity.

31
00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:54.267
Misconduct.

32
00:01:55.283 --> 00:01:58.885
Misconduct means unethical or improper professional behaviour.

33
00:01:59.483 --> 00:02:02.586
Fabricating data is the gravest form of misconduct.

34
00:02:03.183 --> 00:02:05.731
Your turn. Say: Misconduct.

35
00:02:08.567 --> 00:02:09.812
Misconduct.

36
00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:11.493
Source.

37
00:02:12.483 --> 00:02:15.725
Source means the origin of information used in research.

38
00:02:16.317 --> 00:02:19.036
Primary sources are preferred to secondary ones.

39
00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.395
Your turn. Say: Source.

40
00:02:25.233 --> 00:02:26.327
Source.

41
00:02:27.083 --> 00:02:28.340
Accountability.

42
00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:33.962
Accountability means the obligation to accept responsibility for one's actions.

43
00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:37.002
Clear authorship ensures accountability.

44
00:02:37.617 --> 00:02:39.944
Your turn. Say: Accountability.

45
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:43.987
Accountability.

46
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.462
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:49.317 --> 00:02:55.510
Visal, a classmate told me that changing a few words means you don't need a citation. Is that true?

48
00:02:56.617 --> 00:03:05.132
Not at all. Paraphrasing without attribution is still plagiarism, even if every single word is different.

49
00:03:06.217 --> 00:03:09.447
So why do universities treat it so seriously?

50
00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:19.089
Because citing sources properly demonstrates academic integrity. Taking credit for others' ideas undermines the whole system.

51
00:03:20.183 --> 00:03:23.263
What practical advice would you give first-year students?

52
00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:31.948
Recording every source while you read. Reconstructing references the night before a deadline is how mistakes happen.

53
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.751
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:35.617 --> 00:03:38.115
Grammar focus: Gerunds as subjects.

55
00:03:38.415 --> 00:03:39.649
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:43.847
Citing sources properly demonstrates academic integrity.

57
00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:47.620
Paraphrasing without attribution is still plagiarism.

58
00:03:48.517 --> 00:03:51.770
Reconstructing references at the last minute leads to errors.

59
00:03:52.733 --> 00:04:01.260
A gerund phrase can act as a noun subject. However long the phrase, the verb is singular: 'Citing sources properly demonstrates …', not 'demonstrate'.

60
00:04:02.017 --> 00:04:06.538
Be careful! Don't say: Cite sources properly demonstrate integrity.

61
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.067
Say: Citing sources properly demonstrates integrity.

62
00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:14.773
A gerund subject takes a singular verb.

63
00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:18.277
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:20.474
Credit Where It Is Due.

65
00:04:20.774 --> 00:04:25.084
Scholarship is a conversation in which every contribution must be acknowledged.

66
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:31.896
Citing sources properly demonstrates academic integrity and allows readers to verify claims.

67
00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:37.523
Paraphrasing a text, however skilfully, does not remove the obligation of attribution.

68
00:04:38.533 --> 00:04:43.668
Treating plagiarism as a minor offence would devalue degrees and erode public trust in research.

69
00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:50.590
Ultimately, keeping meticulous records is the most reliable defence against accidental misconduct.

70
00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:55.481
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:56.317 --> 00:04:59.640
blank sources properly demonstrates academic integrity.

72
00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.698
A, Cite. B, Cited. C, Citing.

73
00:05:07.233 --> 00:05:09.070
The answer is C: Citing.

74
00:05:10.217 --> 00:05:13.331
Paraphrasing without attribution blank still plagiarism.

75
00:05:13.631 --> 00:05:16.344
A, are. B, is. C, be.

76
00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:21.528
The answer is B: is.

77
00:05:22.683 --> 00:05:26.436
Restating an idea fully in your own words and structure is to blank it.

78
00:05:26.736 --> 00:05:31.272
A, paraphrase. B, plagiarise. C, attribute.

79
00:05:34.833 --> 00:05:37.158
The answer is A: paraphrase.

80
00:05:38.317 --> 00:05:42.360
Which is the gravest form of research misconduct, according to the examples?

81
00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:48.380
A, fabricating data. B, using secondary sources. C, citing images.

82
00:05:51.933 --> 00:05:54.513
The answer is A: fabricating data.

83
00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:58.927
According to the reading, why must sources be cited?

84
00:05:59.227 --> 00:06:05.585
A, so readers can verify claims. B, to make essays longer. C, to avoid paraphrasing.

85
00:06:09.133 --> 00:06:12.201
The answer is A: so readers can verify claims.

86
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.447
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:17.283 --> 00:06:21.117
Why do universities take citation ethics so seriously?

88
00:06:37.917 --> 00:06:39.417
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:39.717 --> 00:07:14.165
Universities take citation ethics seriously because academic knowledge depends on trust. Citing sources allows readers to check claims and gives credit to the people who did the original work. For instance, a degree would lose its value if students could simply copy others' ideas. Some students feel the rules are overly strict, especially for minor errors. Nevertheless, upholding integrity protects everyone, including honest students.

90
00:07:14.465 --> 00:07:16.279
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:17.183 --> 00:07:21.426
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and gerunds as subjects.

92
00:07:21.726 --> 00:07:26.526
Today's value is integrity. Do the right thing, even when no one is watching.

93
00:07:26.826 --> 00:07:29.444
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:29.744 --> 00:07:32.303
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

